---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CopyCode from '../../components/CopyCode.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="CAIL Pi - CUNY AI Lab"
  description="CAIL Pi is the Lab's setup package for Pi, a terminal coding agent, configured to use CUNY AI Lab models."
>
  <section class="relative pt-24 pb-10 lg:pt-28 lg:pb-12">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-br from-cuny-blue via-cuny-blue to-vibrant-800"></div>
    <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-[0.04]" aria-hidden="true" style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=&quot;40&quot; height=&quot;40&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 40 40&quot; xmlns=&quot;http://www.w3.org/2000/svg&quot;%3E%3Cpath d=&quot;M20 20.5V18H0v-2h20v-2.5L25 18l-5 2.5z&quot; fill=&quot;%23ffffff&quot; fill-opacity=&quot;1&quot;/%3E%3C/svg%3E');"></div>
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 relative z-10">
      <a href={`${base}resources/`} class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-vibrant-300 hover:text-white text-sm font-medium mb-4 transition-colors">
        <svg class="w-4 h-4" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 19l-7-7m0 0l7-7m-7 7h18" />
        </svg>
        Back to Resources
      </a>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold font-display text-white tracking-tight">CAIL Pi</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-vibrant-200 text-lg max-w-2xl">Pi is an open-source coding agent that runs in your terminal. CAIL Pi installs it for you and connects it to CUNY AI Lab models with your personal API key.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-neutral-cream">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-10 lg:py-14">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_15rem] gap-8 items-start">
        <article class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 shadow-sm overflow-hidden">
          <div class="p-6 md:p-9 space-y-10">
            <section aria-labelledby="what-heading">
              <h2 id="what-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">What it is</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">Pi works a lot like Claude Code, but it's open source and can talk to any model provider. CAIL Pi points it at the Lab's gateway and saves your CAIL API key, so whatever you do in Pi comes out of your monthly allowance. You don't have to keep a model list up to date: Pi asks the gateway what's available every time it starts.</p>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">The code is on <a href="https://github.com/CUNY-AI-Lab/cail-pi" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-vibrant-700 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-vibrant-800">GitHub</a> and the package is on <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@cuny-ai-lab/cail-pi" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-vibrant-700 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-vibrant-800">npm</a>.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="prereq-heading">
              <h2 id="prereq-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Before you start</h2>
              <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                <li>Your personal CAIL API key. Once your Lab application is approved, create one in <a href="https://tools.ailab.gc.cuny.edu/model-access" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-vibrant-700 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-vibrant-800">your dashboard</a>. The <a href={`${base}docs/api-keys`} class="text-vibrant-700 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-vibrant-800">API key guide</a> walks you through it.</li>
                <li>Node.js 22.19 or newer. Type <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">node --version</code> to check. On Windows you can install it with <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">winget install OpenJS.NodeJS.LTS</code>; afterward, close PowerShell and open a new window so it can find Node. On macOS, use the installer from <a href="https://nodejs.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-vibrant-700 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-vibrant-800">nodejs.org</a>, Homebrew, or nvm. On Linux, use your package manager, nvm, or the official binaries.</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="install-heading">
              <h2 id="install-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Install</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Open a terminal and run the command for your system.</p>
              <CopyCode label="Install on macOS or Linux">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>npx @cuny-ai-lab/cail-pi</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <div class="h-4"></div>
              <CopyCode label="Install on Windows PowerShell">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>npx.cmd @cuny-ai-lab/cail-pi</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4 mb-2">Here's what the installer does:</p>
              <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1.5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                <li>Checks that you have Node.js 22.19 or newer and npm.</li>
                <li>Runs LazyPi, a community installer by Rob Zolkos, which installs Pi and a set of Pi packages. If you already have a Pi older than 1.0, the installer updates it.</li>
                <li>Adds the CUNY AI Lab provider to Pi, and removes the older pi-workshop package if you have it.</li>
                <li>On Windows, turns on Pi's PowerShell tool in Pi's settings file. It backs the file up first. If you've already picked which tools Pi uses, it adds PowerShell to your list and leaves the rest alone.</li>
                <li>Asks for your CAIL API key and checks it with the Lab's gateway. The check looks up your quota instead of calling a model, so it costs nothing. The key is then saved in Pi's credential store on your computer.</li>
                <li>Starts Pi once in the background to load the list of CUNY AI Lab models.</li>
              </ol>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">No key handy? Press Enter at the prompt and setup finishes anyway. Later, run <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/login</code> inside Pi and choose CUNY AI Lab.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="start-heading">
              <h2 id="start-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Start Pi and pick a model</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Go to the folder you want to work in and start Pi. On Windows, use a new PowerShell window so it has the updated PATH.</p>
              <CopyCode label="Start Pi on macOS or Linux">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>pi</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <div class="h-4"></div>
              <CopyCode label="Start Pi on Windows PowerShell">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>pi.cmd</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">Inside Pi, type <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/model</code> and pick a CUNY AI Lab model. Every request counts against your monthly allowance, and <a href="https://tools.ailab.gc.cuny.edu/model-access" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-vibrant-700 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-vibrant-800">your dashboard</a> shows how much is left.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="update-heading">
              <h2 id="update-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Updating Pi</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Pi 1.0 came out on October 1, 2026, and CAIL Pi now requires it. If you installed Pi before then, you need to update once. Close any window where Pi is running, then run:</p>
              <CopyCode label="Update Pi on macOS or Linux">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>pi update --all</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <div class="h-4"></div>
              <CopyCode label="Update Pi on Windows PowerShell">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>pi.cmd update --all</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4 mb-4">That updates Pi and all of your Pi packages, CAIL Pi included. Your key, settings, and old sessions stay as they are. To check that it worked, look at the version number, which should be 1.0.0 or higher, and then run the health check under <a href="#trouble-heading" class="text-vibrant-700 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-vibrant-800">Troubleshooting</a>.</p>
              <CopyCode label="Check the Pi version">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>pi --version       # macOS / Linux
pi.cmd --version   # Windows</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">Running the <a href="#install-heading" class="text-vibrant-700 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-vibrant-800">installer</a> again works too. It updates Pi if it's older than 1.0, updates CAIL Pi, and asks whether you want to keep your saved key.</p>
              <dl class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">Pi takes over the whole terminal window</dt><dd>That's new in 1.0. If you'd rather keep your terminal's normal scrollback, add <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">"tuiMode": "regular"</code> to Pi's settings file (<code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">~/.pi/agent/settings.json</code>, or <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">%USERPROFILE%\.pi\agent\settings.json</code> on Windows), or start Pi with <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">pi --tui-mode regular</code>.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">Pi prints warnings about other extensions when it starts</dt><dd>Some of the community packages LazyPi installs haven't caught up with Pi 1.0. You may see "Host-provided extension packages must be declared in peerDependencies", or a note that Pi switched off its built-in MCP support because <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">pi-mcp-adapter</code> is installed. Neither comes from CAIL Pi, and Pi still works.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">"cannot self-update this installation"</dt><dd>Pi couldn't work out how it was installed, so update it with npm: <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">npm install -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent@latest</code> (<code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">npm.cmd</code> on Windows). Then run <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">pi update --extensions</code> (<code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">pi.cmd update --extensions</code> on Windows) to update your packages.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="trouble-heading">
              <h2 id="trouble-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Troubleshooting</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Start with the health check. It looks at Node, npm, your Pi version, the CUNY AI Lab extension, your saved key, whether the gateway answers and accepts the key, and how many models you can use. On Windows it also checks that Pi's PowerShell tool is on. It never prints your key.</p>
              <CopyCode label="Health check">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>npx @cuny-ai-lab/cail-pi --doctor       # macOS / Linux
npx.cmd @cuny-ai-lab/cail-pi --doctor   # Windows</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <dl class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">"requires Node.js 22.19.0 or newer"</dt><dd>The message names the <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">node</code> that ran. If you have more than one (nvm, Homebrew, conda, and winget can each install their own), switch to a recent one and run the installer again.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">"That API key was not accepted"</dt><dd>Look for missing or extra characters and paste it again. Keys are personal, so make sure it's the one issued to you.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">"The CUNY AI Lab service could not be reached"</dt><dd>Your key wasn't saved. Check your connection (a VPN, a Wi-Fi sign-in page, or a firewall can all get in the way) and choose Retry.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">No CUNY AI Lab models in /model</dt><dd>Pi loads the model list when it starts. If the list stays empty, run the health check. If it says no credential is configured, run <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/login</code> inside Pi and choose CUNY AI Lab.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">PowerShell says scripts are disabled</dt><dd>Add <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">.cmd</code> to the command: <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">npx.cmd</code>, <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">npm.cmd</code>, <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">pi.cmd</code>. You don't need to change the execution policy.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">Windows: "pi is not recognized" after install</dt><dd>Close the window, open a new PowerShell window, and run <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">pi.cmd</code>.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">You installed the earlier pi-workshop package</dt><dd>Run the installer once. It swaps pi-workshop for CAIL Pi so the provider isn't loaded twice, and it keeps your saved key.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="other-heading">
              <h2 id="other-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Other commands</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">To update CAIL Pi by itself, without touching Pi or your other packages:</p>
              <CopyCode label="Update the package">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>pi update npm:@cuny-ai-lab/cail-pi</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4 mb-4">To remove CAIL Pi but keep your saved key:</p>
              <CopyCode label="Remove the package">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>pi remove npm:@cuny-ai-lab/cail-pi</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">On Windows, type <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">pi.cmd</code> instead of <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">pi</code>. To delete the key as well, run the installer with <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">--uninstall</code>, which asks before it deletes anything, or type <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/logout</code> inside Pi.</p>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">The installer takes a few more options: <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">--skip-lazypi</code> if Pi is already installed, <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">--replace-key</code> to enter a new key, <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">--skip-auth</code> to set up without a key, and <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">--no-windows-settings</code> to leave Pi's PowerShell setting alone on Windows.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="privacy-heading">
              <h2 id="privacy-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Privacy and security</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">You type your key at a hidden prompt. The installer never takes it as a command-line argument, so it can't end up in your shell history. The key goes to two places: the Lab's gateway, which checks it, and Pi's credential store on your computer. It is never written to settings files, shell profiles, logs, or error messages, and the health check never shows it. CAIL Pi sends no telemetry.</p>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">Keep your key to yourself. If it leaks, the Lab can revoke it and issue you a new one.</p>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">If you prefer, you can set the <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">AILAB_API_KEY</code> environment variable instead. The provider only uses it when Pi has no saved key.</p>
            </section>
          </div>
        </article>

        <aside class="lg:sticky lg:top-24 bg-white rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 shadow-sm p-5" aria-label="Guide sections">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-neutral-stone mb-4">On this page</h2>
          <nav>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
              <li><a href="#what-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">What it is</a></li>
              <li><a href="#prereq-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Before you start</a></li>
              <li><a href="#install-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Install</a></li>
              <li><a href="#start-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Start Pi</a></li>
              <li><a href="#update-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Updating Pi</a></li>
              <li><a href="#trouble-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Troubleshooting</a></li>
              <li><a href="#other-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Other commands</a></li>
              <li><a href="#privacy-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Privacy and security</a></li>
            </ul>
          </nav>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
